  "track.animation.m.vector.title": "A spec does not know types: everything becomes an AnimationVector",
  "track.animation.m.vector.overview": "Four animated values: a `Dp` with a tween, an `Offset` with a spring, a `Color` with a tween and a `Float` with keyframes. Follow the path at the pinned commit: value → `TwoWayConverter` → `AnimationVector` (1–4 dimensions) → a `VectorizedAnimationSpec` computes each component over time → converted back to the original type.",
  "track.animation.m.vector.wrapup": "`AnimationSpec<T>` has only one job: `vectorize(converter)` into a `VectorizedAnimationSpec<V>` that works on vectors of floats. That is why the same `tween` works for Dp, Offset, Color or your own type, as long as there is a converter.\nColours are animated in the Oklab colour space (four dimensions: alpha, L, a, b), not RGB. So red to white does not have to pass through muddy colours in between.\nRule of thumb: the spec only knows numbers; the converter turns your type into numbers and back.",
  "track.animation.m.vector.simp.1": "Mid-way values: Dp and Color use FastOutSlowIn at 150/300 ms (fraction ≈ 0.776); Offset (spring) numbers are illustrative; keyframes interpolate linearly (LinearEasing is the default between marks).",
  "track.animation.m.vector.simp.2": "The Oklab coordinates of red and white follow the standard Oklab formula from sRGB; the real converter also clamps values and converts back to the target colour space.",
  "track.animation.m.vector.ctl.hint": "Change the value type and watch the vector's dimension.",
